	abstract = {South Africa faces persistent rural-urban energy access gaps due to high grid extension costs and Eskom's instability. Standalone renewable energy systems (SRES) offer viable off-grid solutions. This review assesses SRES through literature, modelling for Qawukeni Village, and case studies of De Aar Solar, Upper Blinkwater, and Maphephetheni. Despite technical promise, progress is hindered by weak policy alignment and limited community participation. Findings highlight the importance of hybrid systems, local engagement, and integrated financing. Lessons from Kenya and Pakistan support policy reforms for a more inclusive rhgffral energy transition.  © 2025 IEEE.},
