	title = {Demand Stimulation for Sustainability of Minigrids in Global South-a Case Study from Zambia},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 IEEE Power Electronics Society International Decentralized Energy Access Symposium, IDEAS 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/IDEAS62193.2025.11157276},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105019047819?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Customized Energy Solutions, Director: Energy Access and Livelihoods, Lusaka, Zambia; Technical Univesity of Munich, Post Doctoral Fellow, School of Management, Munich, Germany},
	abstract = {Most of the existing minigrids in the Global South are struggling to achieve financial sustainability due to a complex mix of factors, including little to no subsidy from governments, lack of transparent policies on grid extension, and minimal support from Development Financial Institutions. One equally relevant factor is the low purchasing power of rural customers and their low energy consumption. Productive Use of Energy (PUE) is a well-known mechanism for stimulating electricity demand in the context of minigrids, and it is considered a promising strategy for achieving mini-grid sustainability at the site level, as well as supporting access to modern energy and the socio-economic development of remote rural regions. This paper describes the case of CES and Engie in the Eastern Province of Zambia and presents a holistic approach to PUE based on community asset mapping. It also illustrates the timeline for demand uptake in minigrids. Based on the case study, we develop recommendations and a preliminary process model for the introduction of PUE in rural communities, with the objective of achieving mini-grid sustainability and positive social, environmental, and economic impact for the parties involved.  © 2025 IEEE.},
